    question:
      "i. In RPC based applications, two important issues that need to be considered for every management are server implementation and server creation. Distinguish between the two concepts. [12 Marks]\nii. Explain in detail the architecture and principles of Monolithic kernel and Micro kernel. [4 Marks Each]\niii. List the five (5) elements of program that are involved in the implementation of an RPC mechanism. [5 Marks]",
    points: ["Stateful vs Stateless", "Kernel architectures", "RPC elements"],
    answer:
      "i. Server implementation vs server creation:\n• Server implementation refers to *how the server behaves*: whether it keeps state (stateful) or handles each request independently (stateless). Stateless servers keep no client info, so recovery is simple. Stateful servers remember client details (open files, positions), giving better performance but harder recovery.\n• Server creation refers to how clients *find and connect* to a server. This includes registering the server with a binder/registry, looking up server locations, binding to the server, and managing issues like load balancing and versioning.\n\nii. Monolithic vs Microkernel architecture:\n• Monolithic kernel:\n  - All core OS services (process management, memory, file system, drivers) run inside the kernel.\n  - Fast because everything communicates through direct function calls.\n  - Less flexible and harder to maintain because everything is tightly connected.\n  - Examples: Traditional UNIX, early Linux.\n\n• Microkernel:\n  - Only essential services (IPC, basic scheduling, low-level memory management) run in the kernel.\n  - Higher-level services like file systems and drivers run in user space.\n  - More modular, easier to extend, and more fault-tolerant.\n  - Slower than monolithic kernels due to more IPC overhead.\n  - Examples: Mach, Chorus, QNX.\n\niii. Five elements of an RPC mechanism:\n1. Client – the process requesting a remote operation.\n2. Client Stub – packs (marshals) parameters and sends the request.\n3. RPC Runtime – handles communication, timeouts, and message delivery.\n4. Server Stub – unpacks parameters and calls the actual server function.\n5. Server – performs the real work and returns results.\nThese components work together to make remote calls appear like normal local function calls.",
