    question:
      "i. With use of clear examples, explain how location, relocation and migration transparencies differ from each other. [15 Marks]\nii. Briefly discuss the security aspects of a Distributed Systems. [5 Marks]\niii. Discuss the issues involved in building a DSM system on a network of heterogeneous machines? [5 Marks]",
    points: ["Transparencies", "Security", "Heterogeneity"],
    answer:
      "i. Location, migration and relocation transparency:\n• Location transparency – Users access a resource without knowing where it is physically located. Example: Accessing a shared file using /shared/docs without knowing the server.\n• Migration transparency – A resource may move to another machine but its name stays the same. Example: A file moved to a new server but path remains unchanged.\n• Relocation transparency – The resource moves *while* it is in use, and the user does not notice. Example: A mobile phone call continues without interruption even as the device switches between cell towers.\nLocation hides the address, migration hides movement, relocation hides movement during use.\n\nii. Security aspects in distributed systems:\nDistributed systems face security challenges because data travels over networks and resources are spread across machines. Key aspects include:\n• Authentication – Ensuring users/services are who they claim to be.\n• Authorization – Controlling access to resources.\n• Confidentiality – Preventing eavesdropping (encryption).\n• Integrity – Ensuring data is not tampered with.\n• Non-repudiation – Actions cannot be denied later.\nSince there's no single trusted machine, security must be enforced at many points.\n\niii. DSM on heterogeneous machines:\nBuilding DSM across different machines is difficult because:\n• Different data formats – Machines may have different byte orders (big/little endian) and data representations.\n• Different page sizes – DSM block size must adapt when machines have different virtual memory page sizes.\n• Different architectures – 32-bit vs 64-bit address spaces.\n• Different operating systems – Different memory management behaviors.\n\nCommon solutions:\n• Use a standard data format like XDR for data conversion.\n• Choose the smallest common page size or use an intermediate block size.\n• Use logical addresses instead of physical pointers.\n• Add a DSM software layer that hides OS differences.\nThese help ensure consistent shared memory behavior across mixed hardware.",
