    unit: "Distributed Systems",
    question:
      "i. Differentiate between time-sharing, parallel processing, network and distributes operating systems. [2 Marks Each]\nii. In what respect are distributed computing systems better than parallel processing systems? [5 Marks]\niii. Discuss the main guiding principles that a distributed operating system designer must keep in mind for good performance of the system? [7 Marks]\niv. What are the major issues of designing a Distributed OS? [5 Marks]",
    points: ["System types", "Advantages", "Design principles", "Issues"],
    answer:
      "i. Differences between system types:\n• Time-sharing OS – One computer is shared by many users. Users think they each have a dedicated machine, but the CPU is switching between tasks.\n• Parallel processing OS – Multiple processors share a single memory (tightly coupled). Processors work together on one task.\n• Network OS – Many independent computers connected by a network. Users must know which machine stores which resources.\n• Distributed OS – Many computers appear as one system. Location transparency hides where files, processes, or resources are.\n\n\nii. Why distributed systems are better than parallel systems:\n• Scalability – Easy to add new nodes using normal hardware.\n• Fault tolerance – If one node fails, others continue working.\n• Geographic distribution – Nodes can be in different locations.\n• Cost – Uses inexpensive networked machines instead of special shared-memory hardware.\n• Flexibility – Supports different types of machines and operating systems.\n\n\niii. Guiding principles for good performance:\n• Minimize network communication – Move computation to data where possible.\n• Cache frequently used data to avoid repeated network transfers.\n• Batch operations – Group small messages together.\n• Reduce copying – Use zero-copy techniques.\n• Use parallelism – Allow multiple threads/processes to run concurrently.\n• Balance load – Spread work across nodes to avoid bottlenecks.\n• Minimize synchronization – Too much locking slows the system.\n\n\niv. Major issues in designing a distributed OS:\n• Transparency – Hiding distribution details (location, migration, replication, etc.).\n• Scalability – System should work efficiently as nodes increase.\n• Reliability – Must tolerate node and network failures.\n• Security – Must protect data across untrusted networks.\n• Resource management – Scheduling, load sharing, and process migration.\n• Communication – Efficient message passing/RPC mechanisms.\n• Synchronization – Global clocks, mutual exclusion, deadlock handling.",
