    answer:
      "RPC PRINCIPLES:\n\n1. Basic Concept:\n- Remote Procedure Call allows calling procedures on remote machines as if local\n- Provides procedural abstraction over message passing\n- Goal: Make distributed computing look like local computing\n\n2. Key Principles:\n\na) Location Transparency:\n- Client doesn't know procedure is remote\n- Same syntax as local procedure call\n- Hides network communication details\n\nb) Interface Definition:\n- Procedures defined in Interface Definition Language (IDL)\n- Specifies procedure names, parameters, return types\n- Client and server agree on interface contract\n\nc) Client-Server Model:\n- Client: Process that calls remote procedure\n- Server: Process that implements procedure\n- Asymmetric relationship\n\nd) Synchronous Communication:\n- Client blocks after making call\n- Waits for server to execute and reply\n- Resumes execution after receiving result\n\n3. RPC Mechanism:\n\nClient Side:\n1. Client calls procedure (appears local)\n2. Client Stub intercepts call\n3. Stub marshals parameters (packs into message)\n4. Stub calls local OS to send message\n5. Client blocks waiting for reply\n6. OS receives reply message\n7. Stub unmarshals results\n8. Stub returns results to client\n9. Client continues execution\n\nServer Side:\n1. Server OS receives request message\n2. Server Stub unmarshals parameters\n3. Stub calls actual server procedure\n4. Server executes procedure\n5. Server returns result to stub\n6. Stub marshals return values\n7. Stub calls OS to send reply\n8. OS sends reply to client\n\n4. Core Components:\n\na) Stubs:\n- Client Stub: Proxy on client side, represents server procedure\n- Server Stub: Skeleton on server side, receives calls\n- Purpose: Hide communication complexity, provide transparency\n\nb) Marshaling/Unmarshaling:\n- Convert procedure parameters to transmittable format\n- Handle data representation differences (endianness, types)\n- Pack/unpack complex data structures\n\nc) RPC Runtime:\n- Handles message transmission\n- Manages network protocols\n- Provides reliability (retransmissions, acknowledgments)\n- Handles server location and binding\n\n5. Communication Semantics:\n\na) At-Least-Once:\n- Client retransmits if no reply\n- Server may execute multiple times\n- Suitable for idempotent operations\n\nb) At-Most-Once:\n- Server detects duplicates\n- Executes procedure at most once\n- Requires duplicate detection mechanism\n\nc) Exactly-Once:\n- Ideal but difficult to achieve\n- Requires transactional mechanisms\n- High overhead\n\n6. Binding Process:\n- Client must locate server\n- Name server (binder) maintains service registry\n- Server exports interface to binder\n- Client imports interface from binder\n- Dynamic binding allows server relocation\n\nDESIGN ISSUES OF DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS:\n\n1. TRANSPARENCY (8 Types):\n\na) Access Transparency:\n- Hide differences in data representation\n- Uniform access to local and remote resources\n- Challenge: Different hardware architectures\n\nb) Location Transparency:\n- Users don't need to know resource location\n- Resources accessed by logical names\n- Challenge: Naming schemes, name resolution\n\nc) Migration Transparency:\n- Resources can move without name change\n- System reconfiguration hidden from users\n- Challenge: Maintaining connections during migration\n\nd) Relocation Transparency:\n- Resources can move while being accessed\n- Active sessions continue during movement\n- Challenge: State transfer, redirection mechanisms\n\ne) Replication Transparency:\n- Multiple copies hidden from users\n- System manages replicas automatically\n- Challenge: Consistency maintenance\n\nf) Concurrency Transparency:\n- Multiple users can share resources\n- Concurrent access appears sequential\n- Challenge: Locking, serialization overhead\n\ng) Failure Transparency:\n- System continues despite component failures\n- Faults hidden from users\n- Challenge: Detecting and recovering from failures\n\nh) Performance Transparency:\n- System reconfigures for improved performance\n- Load balancing automatic\n- Challenge: Detecting bottlenecks, dynamic reconfiguration\n\n2. RELIABILITY:\n\na) Fault Tolerance:\n- System continues despite failures\n- Techniques: Redundancy, replication, checkpointing\n- Challenge: Cost vs benefit tradeoff\n\nb) Availability:\n- System accessible when needed\n- Measured as uptime percentage\n- Challenge: Balancing availability with cost\n\nc) Recovery:\n- Restore system after failure\n- Backward recovery (checkpoints) or Forward recovery\n- Challenge: State consistency after recovery\n\nd) Redundancy:\n- Information redundancy (error detection/correction)\n- Time redundancy (retry operations)\n- Physical redundancy (backup components)\n\n3. PERFORMANCE:\n\na) Response Time:\n- Time from request to response\n- Affected by: Network latency, server load, queuing\n- Challenge: Minimizing delay in distributed environment\n\nb) Throughput:\n- Number of operations per unit time\n- Affected by: Bandwidth, processing capacity\n- Challenge: Achieving high throughput with distribution overhead\n\nc) Load Balancing:\n- Distribute work evenly across nodes\n- Static or dynamic algorithms\n- Challenge: Overhead of migration vs benefits\n\n4. SCALABILITY:\n\na) Size Scalability:\n- Add more users and resources without performance loss\n- Challenge: Centralized components become bottlenecks\n- Solution: Decentralization, distribution\n\nb) Geographic Scalability:\n- Span large geographic areas\n- Challenge: Network latency, heterogeneity\n- Solution: Caching, replication\n\nc) Administrative Scalability:\n- Span multiple administrative domains\n- Challenge: Different policies, security models\n- Solution: Hierarchical organization\n\nScalability Techniques:\n- Avoid centralized services (single points of failure)\n- Decentralized algorithms (no global state collection)\n- Asynchronous communication (avoid waiting)\n- Caching and replication (reduce communication)\n\n5. SECURITY:\n\na) Authentication:\n- Verify identity of users and processes\n- Challenge: No single trusted authority\n- Solution: Cryptographic protocols, certificates\n\nb) Authorization:\n- Control access to resources\n- Challenge: Distributed policy enforcement\n- Solution: Capabilities, Access Control Lists\n\nc) Confidentiality:\n- Protect data from eavesdropping\n- Challenge: Network exposure\n- Solution: Encryption\n\nd) Integrity:\n- Ensure data not tampered with\n- Challenge: Multiple communication hops\n- Solution: Digital signatures, checksums\n\n6. HETEROGENEITY:\n\na) Hardware Heterogeneity:\n- Different processor architectures\n- Different byte orders (endianness)\n- Solution: Standard data representations (XDR)\n\nb) Software Heterogeneity:\n- Different operating systems\n- Different programming languages\n- Solution: Middleware, standard interfaces\n\nc) Network Heterogeneity:\n- Different network protocols\n- Different network types (LAN, WAN)\n- Solution: Protocol stacks, gateways\n\n7. OPENNESS:\n- Publish interfaces and protocols\n- Allow interoperability\n- Support extensibility\n- Challenge: Ensuring compliance with standards\n\n8. CONCURRENCY:\n- Multiple processes access shared resources\n- Need for synchronization\n- Challenge: No shared memory, no global clock\n- Solution: Distributed locking, timestamps, consensus algorithms",
